$string['condition:budget:error:requests'] = 'A budget counted in requests has to be a whole number of them.';
$string['condition:budget:error:retention'] = 'This budget looks back {$a->reach} days, but the site keeps its daily summaries for only {$a->kept}. The older part of the period would be thrown away while the budget was still counting it, so the spending would read lower than it was. Shorten the budget period, or raise the summary retention on the AI usage settings screen first.';
$string['condition:budget:error:scope'] = 'Choose whose spending this is about, or clear the amount to leave the budget condition out.';
$string['condition:budget:metric:cost'] = 'spending';
$string['condition:budget:metric:requests'] = 'request count';
$string['condition:budget:over'] = 'has reached';
$string['condition:budget:period:month'] = 'this calendar month';
$string['condition:budget:period:rolling'] = 'in the last';
$string['condition:budget:provider:choose'] = 'Choose a provider...';
$string['condition:budget:requests'] = '{$a} requests';
$string['condition:budget:scope:course'] = 'The course';
$string['condition:budget:scope:none'] = 'No budget condition';
$string['condition:budget:scope:site'] = 'The site';
$string['condition:budget:scope:user'] = 'The person';
$string['condition:budget:under'] = 'is under';
$string['condition:budget:unit:requests'] = 'requests';
$string['condition:budget_help'] = 'Only spending the site paid for is counted. Requests covered by a key somebody brought cost the site nothing, and an owner who wants to limit their own key does that on the key itself. A budget is counted either in money or in requests. Money is counted at one provider, in the currency that provider bills in, and comes from the rates entered for it: money is never added across providers, so a budget for all of the site\'s AI is a budget at each provider, and a model with no rate contributes nothing. Spending that cannot be worked out satisfies this condition neither way round. A count of requests is always available and counts every request whatever provider answered it, which is the measure to use for models that cost nothing to run and for an allowance a provider writes in requests. There is no setting for what to do when a budget runs out, because the order of the rules already says it: put the rule needing room above a rule with no conditions to fall back on something cheaper, or leave nothing below it so the request is refused. Figures are held for up to a minute, so a burst of requests can carry spending slightly past the limit.';
